WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.320 --> 00:00:02.640
- [Narrator] These satellite images

00:00:02.640 --> 00:00:03.960
show the Engels air base,

00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:05.910
a strategic hub for Russian forces,

00:00:05.910 --> 00:00:09.240
before and after it was hit by a strike.

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:11.820
Following the explosion,
there are fewer aircraft left

00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:14.160
and what looks like damage to the tarmac.

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:15.210
The base is located

00:00:15.210 --> 00:00:18.690
more than 350 miles away
from the Ukrainian border.

00:00:18.690 --> 00:00:20.130
It's one of multiple blasts

00:00:20.130 --> 00:00:21.960
that have recently hit military bases

00:00:21.960 --> 00:00:24.300
and fuel storage sites in Russia

00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:26.130
as well as Russian troops in Ukraine,

00:00:26.130 --> 00:00:29.130
including the deadliest
known strike in months.

00:00:29.130 --> 00:00:31.800
The attacks highlight how
combat is not just playing out

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:33.183
along the front lines.

00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:35.910
Targeted strikes like this one

00:00:35.910 --> 00:00:37.110
on another Russian air base

00:00:37.110 --> 00:00:39.033
have taken on a big role in the war.

00:00:39.990 --> 00:00:41.520
Aviation and security experts

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:43.470
say the images reveal Russia struggled

00:00:43.470 --> 00:00:45.210
to protect its military assets

00:00:45.210 --> 00:00:47.973
and Ukraine's tactics to
destabilize its enemy.

00:00:49.530 --> 00:00:51.720
The Engels Airfield is
the main operating base

00:00:51.720 --> 00:00:53.553
of long-range aviation in Russia.

00:00:54.570 --> 00:00:56.400
- Engels has been used as a launchpad

00:00:56.400 --> 00:00:57.600
for Russia's near daily

00:00:57.600 --> 00:01:01.290
or sometimes weekly air
raids across Ukraine.

00:01:01.290 --> 00:01:02.580
- [Narrator] In these satellite images

00:01:02.580 --> 00:01:03.810
taken in early December,

00:01:03.810 --> 00:01:06.750
more than 30 Russian war
planes can be seen at the base.

00:01:06.750 --> 00:01:09.060
That includes 19 TU-95s,

00:01:09.060 --> 00:01:12.660
strategic bombers capable
of carrying nuclear weapons.

00:01:12.660 --> 00:01:15.240
Aviation experts from
Janes, an intelligence firm,

00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:18.633
and CNA, a security think
tank, analyzed the images.

00:01:19.470 --> 00:01:21.660
They said the planes are
located closely together

00:01:21.660 --> 00:01:22.493
without cover,

00:01:22.493 --> 00:01:25.140
indicating they aren't
prepared for any type of strike

00:01:26.490 --> 00:01:28.770
- Military, and also, imagine
they must have assumed

00:01:28.770 --> 00:01:33.270
that 350 miles into its
own territory was safe.

00:01:33.270 --> 00:01:35.939
- [Narrator] But shortly after
a first blast hit the base,

00:01:35.939 --> 00:01:38.460
(explosion echoing)

00:01:38.460 --> 00:01:40.320
the sound of the explosion was recorded

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:42.630
by security cameras nearby.

00:01:42.630 --> 00:01:45.420
Russia didn't immediately
respond to a request for comment

00:01:45.420 --> 00:01:46.980
about whether it was prepared.

00:01:46.980 --> 00:01:48.120
Moscow sent a statement

00:01:48.120 --> 00:01:51.000
that Ukrainian drones were
intercepted by its air defenses

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:52.500
and the wreckage hit the facility,

00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:55.020
killing three Russian servicemen.

00:01:55.020 --> 00:01:57.570
Kyiv hasn't claimed
responsibility for the strikes,

00:01:57.570 --> 00:01:59.130
but Ukraine's forces have hinted

00:01:59.130 --> 00:02:01.830
that they're capable of launching them.

00:02:01.830 --> 00:02:04.710
Military analysts said images
taken after the explosion

00:02:04.710 --> 00:02:06.840
show it appears to be firefighting foam

00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:08.190
underneath one bomber

00:02:08.190 --> 00:02:10.413
and a possible impact site next to it.

00:02:11.430 --> 00:02:14.070
Days later, Moscow appeared
to have taken measures

00:02:14.070 --> 00:02:16.650
to try and protect its assets.

00:02:16.650 --> 00:02:17.640
On December 12th,

00:02:17.640 --> 00:02:20.760
pictures show that nine
fewer TU-95 aircraft

00:02:20.760 --> 00:02:21.990
are at the base.

00:02:21.990 --> 00:02:23.880
The Ukrainian Ministry of Defense

00:02:23.880 --> 00:02:26.100
said the planes have been relocated,

00:02:26.100 --> 00:02:28.380
and military analysts
say the wider spacing

00:02:28.380 --> 00:02:30.870
between the planes and
the newly erected barriers

00:02:30.870 --> 00:02:33.930
are strategies to protect
the hub from attacks.

00:02:33.930 --> 00:02:35.490
Russia didn't immediately respond

00:02:35.490 --> 00:02:38.190
to a request for comment
about those planes.

00:02:38.190 --> 00:02:40.710
Weeks later, another security
camera captured the sound

00:02:40.710 --> 00:02:43.510
of a second strike at the same base.

00:02:43.510 --> 00:02:46.830
(explosion echoing)

00:02:46.830 --> 00:02:49.710
With its soldiers being hit
by strikes in military bases

00:02:49.710 --> 00:02:51.930
where they're supposed
to be relatively safe,

00:02:51.930 --> 00:02:54.420
analysts say Ukraine hopes
to undermine the morale

00:02:54.420 --> 00:02:55.710
of Russian troops.

00:02:55.710 --> 00:02:58.980
- This campaign is also part
of another front in the war

00:02:58.980 --> 00:03:01.260
playing away far from the front lines.

00:03:01.260 --> 00:03:02.820
- [Narrator] In Ukraine,
Russia's been shelling

00:03:02.820 --> 00:03:04.860
a number of military
and civilian buildings

00:03:04.860 --> 00:03:06.420
and infrastructure.

00:03:06.420 --> 00:03:07.920
But since the early days of the war,

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:09.060
Kyiv has been working to show

00:03:09.060 --> 00:03:12.210
that it's also capable of
destabilizing the invader.

00:03:12.210 --> 00:03:16.260
- In April, Ukraine hit the the flagship

00:03:16.260 --> 00:03:20.070
of the Russian Black Sea
Fleet, the Moskva, sinking it,

00:03:20.070 --> 00:03:23.040
which was the first big shock
that Ukraine could do this.

00:03:23.040 --> 00:03:26.400
It also hit a bridge that Putin opened

00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:29.010
from Russia to Crimea.

00:03:29.010 --> 00:03:30.780
- [Narrator] Several of
those Ukrainian attacks

00:03:30.780 --> 00:03:32.490
prompted Moscow to retaliate

00:03:32.490 --> 00:03:34.380
with heavy barrages of missiles,

00:03:34.380 --> 00:03:37.353
diverting attention and
resources from the front lines.

00:03:38.760 --> 00:03:41.400
Strikes like this one on
another airfield in Russia

00:03:41.400 --> 00:03:43.020
can also be hard to detect,

00:03:43.020 --> 00:03:45.063
complicating Russia's operations.

00:03:46.020 --> 00:03:48.180
Moscow accused Ukraine
of hitting its bases

00:03:48.180 --> 00:03:51.870
with Soviet-designed jet drones
that fly at a low altitude.

00:03:51.870 --> 00:03:53.220
Military analysts point out

00:03:53.220 --> 00:03:55.080
it's difficult to independently verify

00:03:55.080 --> 00:03:56.700
what weapons were actually used,

00:03:56.700 --> 00:03:58.530
and that makes it more
difficult for Russia

00:03:58.530 --> 00:04:01.503
to anticipate future strikes
on its own territory.

00:04:03.150 --> 00:04:04.260
Russia, on its end,

00:04:04.260 --> 00:04:06.450
also continues to use missiles and drones

00:04:06.450 --> 00:04:09.300
to strike power stations and
other infrastructure in Kyiv

00:04:09.300 --> 00:04:10.950
and other parts of Ukraine,

00:04:10.950 --> 00:04:13.893
causing blackouts and disrupting
life across the country.

00:04:15.210 --> 00:04:17.070
But the strikes hitting its military

00:04:17.070 --> 00:04:18.900
have affected operations.

00:04:18.900 --> 00:04:21.780
And now that bases closer
to Moscow are being hit,

00:04:21.780 --> 00:04:23.970
military analysts say
Russia is being forced

00:04:23.970 --> 00:04:26.343
to spread out its already
stretched defenses.

